# Hand-written worked questions. Correct option is authored first, then shuffled.
worked='''
1|A child understands a sentence never previously heard. Which inference is strongest?|Linguistic knowledge supports productive combinations.|The child has memorized every possible sentence.|Every meaningful sentence must have been heard before.|The child knows every word in every language.|Productivity allows familiar resources to form novel messages. Understanding a new sentence does not require an infinite memory of complete sentences.|Productivity|Moderate
1|Which pair correctly distinguishes two design features?|Displacement: discussing yesterday; duality: combining meaningless sounds into meaningful words.|Displacement: louder speech; duality: whispering.|Displacement: a dictionary definition; duality: a future plan.|Displacement: two synonyms; duality: two speakers.|Displacement moves reference beyond the immediate context. Duality concerns two organizational levels, not simply anything involving two items.|Design features|Moderate
1|A signed language has systematic word formation and sentence structure. What follows?|A visual-gestural language can have a full grammar.|It is merely a substitute alphabet without syntax.|It cannot express absent events.|It must share one universal grammar with every other signed language.|Signed languages are natural linguistic systems. Their modality differs from speech, while their grammatical organization and expressive capacities are substantial.|Signed language|Moderate
1|Two neighbours say Good morning without exchanging new facts. What is the dominant function?|Maintaining social contact.|Giving a scientific definition.|Issuing a legal verdict.|Counting grammatical units.|This is phatic communion: the greeting maintains a relationship. It may also express goodwill, but new factual content is not its dominant purpose.|Language functions|Easy
1|A lecturer says The word plural refers to more than one in this example. Which function dominates?|Metalingual.|Performative declaration.|Recreational wordplay.|Emotive outburst.|The utterance explains linguistic terminology. Language is being used to discuss the code itself, illustrating the metalingual function.|Metalingual function|Easy
1|Which statement best evaluates an old definition limiting language to vocal symbols?|It describes a historical emphasis but excludes natural signed languages.|It proves signed languages have no meaningful symbols.|It makes writing biologically necessary.|It proves all signs imitate their referents.|Historical definitions can be important for examination study while requiring qualification. Contemporary descriptions recognize visual-gestural natural languages.|Historical definitions|Challenging
1|One language has a finite vocabulary and no fixed upper bound on possible sentence length. Which statement is consistent?|Each individual sentence can be finite while the possible set is unbounded.|Every sentence must be infinite.|A finite inventory allows exactly one sentence.|Unbounded productivity eliminates grammatical constraints.|An unbounded collection can contain only finite members. This is central to the distinction between finite linguistic resources and potentially unbounded productivity.|Formal language characterization|Challenging
1|A speaker tells the truth using an ungrammatical word order. What is the best conclusion?|Truth and grammaticality are different dimensions.|Every true statement is grammatically well formed.|Word order cannot affect grammar.|Truth is determined by punctuation alone.|A message can be true yet structurally unacceptable in the intended variety. Conversely, a grammatical sentence can be false or describe an imaginary event.|Language as a system|Moderate
1|A child acquires the language used by caregivers rather than a distant ancestral language. Which feature explains the particular language acquired?|Cultural transmission.|Automatic inheritance of vocabulary.|Alphabetical ordering.|Institutional performativity.|Experience with a linguistic community shapes which language a child acquires. Biological capacities do not transmit a ready-made ancestry-specific vocabulary.|Cultural transmission|Easy
1|A poem expresses sorrow while also using playful rhyme. Which analysis is best?|Language functions can coexist within one text.|Each text must have exactly one function.|Rhyme prevents expression of emotion.|Emotive use excludes all conventional meanings.|Functional labels describe purposes rather than mutually exclusive boxes. The poem can combine emotive expression with enjoyment of patterned language.|Language functions|Moderate
2|Which project most directly belongs to phonology rather than phonetics?|Testing which sound substitutions distinguish words in Urdu.|Measuring a vowel's physical duration.|Tracking the movement of the lips.|Measuring waveform amplitude.|Phonology asks how a language organizes contrasts and patterns. The other projects investigate physical production or signals, though the fields can inform one another.|Phonetics versus phonology|Moderate
2|A team creates a dictionary from corpus evidence. Which pairing is most accurate?|Lexicography as the task, corpus methods as a source of evidence.|Neurolinguistics as the task, brain imaging as the only evidence.|Historical reconstruction as the task, spelling alone as the evidence.|Phonotactics as the task, legal authority as the evidence.|Fields and methods can overlap. Dictionary compilation is lexicography, and corpus analysis can guide definitions, senses, examples and usage labels.|Research methods|Moderate
2|Which pairing correctly identifies a question and its field?|How do listeners recognize words? — psycholinguistics.|How do listeners recognize words? — lexicography only.|How are dictionaries printed? — neurolinguistics only.|How do vowels resonate? — forensic authorship only.|Word recognition is a mental processing question. Methods may include response-time experiments and behavioural measures; neural methods can add a related perspective.|Psycholinguistics|Easy
2|Two unrelated languages both show SOV order. What can be inferred directly?|They share a typological property.|They necessarily descend from one recent ancestor.|They use identical vocabulary.|Their writing systems must be identical.|Typology compares structure. Historical relatedness requires systematic comparative evidence, and shared word order alone does not establish it.|Typology versus genealogy|Challenging
2|A researcher examines how speakers alternate between formal and informal styles. Which variable is central?|Communicative situation and social meaning.|The periodicity of the microphone signal alone.|The number of pages in the dictionary.|The order of letters in the alphabet.|Style and register variation relate linguistic choices to context and social relationships. This is a sociolinguistic concern, even when pronunciation or syntax supplies the features measured.|Sociolinguistics|Moderate
2|What distinguishes a corpus from an unsupported set of illustrative sentences?|A corpus is an organized collection whose selection and design can be examined.|A corpus must contain every sentence ever spoken.|A corpus automatically removes all sampling bias.|A corpus proves that absent expressions are impossible.|Corpus evidence has a traceable collection design. Its representativeness and limitations must be considered before generalizing frequencies or absence.|Corpus linguistics|Challenging
2|Which project is clearly a computational-linguistics task?|Building a system that recognizes spoken Urdu words.|Studying only the grammar of a programming language for its own sake.|Choosing a national flag colour.|Measuring body temperature without any language task.|Automatic speech recognition processes human language computationally. Programming supports the task, but the linguistic object is natural speech.|Computational linguistics|Easy
2|A legal linguist is given a short anonymous message. Which conclusion is most defensible?|Any authorship assessment must acknowledge evidential limits.|The author can always be identified with certainty.|Grammar alone reveals an exact national identity.|A single unusual word proves authorship.|Forensic analysis can compare linguistic evidence, but short samples and shared features limit certainty. Claims must match the strength and scope of the data.|Forensic linguistics|Challenging
2|A poem repeats a consonant sound to create a particular effect. Which combined approach fits?|Literary stylistics using sound-pattern analysis.|Lexicography using only alphabetical order.|Historical linguistics using only an author's birthday.|Language planning using a legal verdict.|Literary stylistics draws on linguistic levels to explain textual effects. Sound repetition supplies evidence that should be connected to an interpretation of the poem.|Literary stylistics|Moderate
2|Which statement about micro- and macrolinguistics is safest?|They are broad organizational labels whose boundaries can vary.|Every textbook assigns pragmatics to exactly the same side.|Macrolinguistics never uses structural evidence.|Microlinguistics means the study of small languages.|The labels are pedagogical classifications. Some work crosses boundaries, and pragmatics is especially variably classified across introductions.|Micro and macro divisions|Challenging
3|Which feature distinguishes [s] from [z] in their ordinary IPA classifications?|Voicing.|Place alone.|Lip rounding alone.|Number of syllables.|Both are alveolar fricatives in a standard classification. [s] is voiceless and [z] voiced, so vocal-fold vibration supplies the main contrast.|Consonant features|Easy
3|Which complete description correctly identifies [m]?|Voiced bilabial nasal.|Voiceless alveolar stop.|Voiced labiodental fricative.|Voiceless velar affricate.|[m] uses both lips for oral closure, voiced phonation in ordinary production, and a lowered velum permitting nasal airflow.|Consonant classification|Moderate
3|Which complete description correctly identifies [k]?|Voiceless velar stop.|Voiced dental nasal.|Voiceless bilabial fricative.|Voiced palatal approximant.|[k] is made with a dorsal closure at the velum and is ordinarily voiceless. Compare voiced [ɡ] and velar nasal [ŋ].|Consonant classification|Moderate
3|How many segments are represented in [spɪn] under the handout's ordinary segmental analysis?|Four.|Two.|Three.|Five.|The representation contains [s], [p], [ɪ] and [n]. A segment count concerns sounds, not letters in every possible spelling system.|Segment counting|Easy
3|Pin and spin show aspirated versus unaspirated realizations of English /p/. What does this primarily illustrate?|Context-conditioned allophony.|Two plural allomorphs.|A minimal pair differing only in aspiration.|A universal ban on aspiration.|The words differ in more than aspiration, so they are not a minimal pair testing aspiration. English aspiration here follows the phonological environment.|Allophony|Challenging
3|A spectrogram has time horizontally and frequency vertically. What does darker shading conventionally indicate?|Greater energy in that time-frequency region.|More grammatical morphemes.|A different dictionary spelling.|A categorical increase in sentence truth.|Shading represents spectral energy under the chosen display settings. It does not directly encode meaning, word class or grammatical correctness.|Spectrogram interpretation|Moderate
3|Which property primarily distinguishes a formant from fundamental frequency?|A formant reflects vocal-tract resonance, while F0 reflects periodicity.|A formant is spelling, while F0 is grammar.|Both are names for the number of letters.|F0 can only be measured in milliseconds.|The source-filter distinction separates laryngeal periodicity from resonances of the vocal tract. F1 and F2 are especially useful for analyzing vowel quality.|Acoustic distinctions|Challenging
3|Which pair provides the clearest English phonemic contrast?|Sip and zip.|Pin and spin.|Teacher and teaching.|Beautiful and attractive.|Sip and zip differ only in the initial /s/ versus /z/, with meaning changing. Pin and spin also differ in the additional /s/, and the remaining pairs are not minimal pairs.|Minimal-pair analysis|Moderate
3|A sound is written [pʰ] rather than /p/. Which information has been added explicitly?|Phonetic aspiration detail.|An obligatory change in lexical meaning.|A plural grammatical meaning.|A new orthographic alphabet.|Brackets indicate phonetic transcription and [ʰ] marks aspiration. Adding phonetic detail does not by itself establish a new phoneme in the language.|Transcription|Moderate
3|Which inference is valid when English disallows a particular initial cluster?|The restriction is a fact about English phonotactics, not proof of a universal impossibility.|The cluster cannot occur in any natural language.|Every speaker is physically unable to articulate it.|The written alphabet determines all permitted clusters.|Languages differ in permitted sound sequences. A sequence absent from one system can be ordinary in another; physical production and linguistic licensing differ.|Phonotactics|Challenging
4|How many morphemes occur in unhelpfulness under the ordinary analysis?|Four.|Two.|Three.|Five.|The units are un- + help + -ful + -ness. Each contributes lexical or grammatical meaning. Syllable count and letter count answer different questions.|Morpheme counting|Moderate
4|Which analysis of teachers is correct?|Teach + derivational -er + inflectional -s.|Teacher + inflectional -ness.|Tea + cher + s as three lexical roots.|Teach + comparative -er + past -s.|Agentive -er forms the noun teacher from teach, then plural -s inflects the derived noun. Comparative -er has a different grammatical function despite matching spelling.|Affix function and order|Challenging
4|Choose the regular English plural ending sequence for cats, dogs and buses.|[s], [z], [ɪz].|[z], [s], [t].|[ɪz], [t], [d].|[d], [t], [z].|A voiceless nonsibilant conditions [s], a voiced nonsibilant conditions [z], and a sibilant conditions a syllabic plural, commonly transcribed [ɪz] or [əz] depending on variety and analysis.|Plural allomorphs|Moderate
4|Choose the past-ending sequence for walked, played and wanted.|[t], [d], [ɪd].|[d], [t], [s].|[ɪd], [s], [z].|[z], [ɪz], [t].|Regular past tense has [t] after voiceless segments other than /t/, [d] after voiced segments other than /d/, and a syllabic form after /t/ or /d/.|Past allomorphs|Moderate
4|Which pair contains the same written suffix with different morphological functions?|Teacher and taller.|Cats and dogs.|Played and walked.|Buses and classes.|The -er of teacher forms an agent noun and is derivational; the -er of taller marks comparative degree and is inflectional. Matching orthography does not guarantee matching morphemes.|Derivation versus inflection|Challenging
4|In the boy from China, what is the category of from China?|Prepositional phrase.|Adjective phrase by head.|Noun phrase headed by boy.|Finite independent clause.|From is the head, so the constituent is a prepositional phrase. It modifies a noun, but adjectival function does not change its head category.|Category versus function|Moderate
4|Which example is a clear adjective phrase by head category?|Very proud of her work.|A proud student.|In a proud manner.|She is proud.|Proud heads very proud of her work. A proud student is a noun phrase, in a proud manner is a prepositional phrase, and She is proud is a clause.|Phrase classification|Moderate
4|Which analysis of men is appropriate in introductory English morphology?|An irregular plural involving internal vowel change.|An ordinary infix -e- added to man.|A regular plural with [s].|A compound of two complete nouns.|The plural relation involves a vowel alternation rather than an inserted affix. Its morphological analysis should not be confused with genuine infixation.|Internal modification|Moderate
4|Which sentence contains a supplementary absolute construction?|Her eyes on the clock, Sara waited.|Sara watched the clock.|Because Sara waited, Ali left.|Sara waited and Ali left.|Her eyes on the clock has its own nominal element and a predicative relation without a finite verb. It supplements the main clause rather than serving as its subject.|Absolute construction|Challenging
4|Which ordering correctly represents units from larger to smaller in a common introductory rank scale?|Sentence, clause, phrase, word, morpheme.|Morpheme, sentence, word, clause, phrase.|Syntax, morpheme, sentence, phonetics, word.|Word, clause, morpheme, phrase, sentence.|The rank scale orders grammatical units. Syntax and phonetics name fields or levels of analysis, not unit ranks parallel to word or clause.|Grammatical ranks|Easy
5|A linguist records a systematic nonstandard agreement pattern. What does a descriptive account do?|Describe its distribution within that variety.|Declare that the variety has no grammar.|Treat social disapproval as proof of structural randomness.|Remove all examples not found in a style manual.|Description investigates what users systematically do. A pattern may be nonstandard in formal writing while remaining rule-governed within a variety.|Descriptive method|Moderate
5|A student recites an agreement rule but repeatedly struggles to apply it in fast conversation. Which distinction is relevant?|Explicit knowledge versus fluent real-time use.|Writing versus the complete absence of knowledge.|Typology versus genealogy only.|Denotation versus homography only.|Conscious rule knowledge does not guarantee automatic performance under time pressure. Processing practice and implicit learning may affect fluent use.|Knowledge and performance|Moderate
5|A child says two foots. Which interpretation is best supported?|The child is extending a regular plural pattern to an irregular noun.|The child has no sensitivity to number.|The child merely repeats the adult form feet exactly.|All English nouns necessarily take -s.|Overregularization reveals productive generalization. Adult feet must also be learned as an irregular form, so the error is informative about learning.|Acquisition|Moderate
5|A long sentence follows proposed grammatical rules but overwhelms working memory. What follows?|Processing difficulty can reduce acceptability without proving a grammatical violation.|Every hard-to-process sentence is structurally impossible.|Memory is irrelevant to language performance.|A grammar must list only short sentences.|Acceptability judgments reflect several factors. Structural licensing and processing limits should be analyzed separately, while both inform actual use.|Grammaticality and acceptability|Challenging
5|Which claim accurately states the status of Universal Grammar?|It is a theoretical proposal whose content and explanatory value are debated.|It is an agreed catalogue of identical words in every language.|It proves that every child acquires language without input.|It is the rulebook for one national standard.|Generative theories propose innate linguistic constraints, but their formulation and evidence are contested. Input and developmental experience remain important.|Universal Grammar|Challenging
5|A researcher compares present-day English and Urdu word order without tracing their histories. The comparison is primarily what?|Synchronic and typological.|Necessarily diachronic reconstruction.|Purely lexicographic printing.|Only auditory anatomy.|Synchronic concerns the period examined; typological concerns structural comparison. These labels can apply together because they classify different aspects of the project.|Analytical perspectives|Moderate
5|Which fact supports treating an individual's slip cautiously as grammatical evidence?|Attention and memory can disrupt actual production.|Every slip is a new rule shared by the whole community.|Mental grammar contains only correctly printed sentences.|Listeners cannot understand imperfect speech.|Performance is affected by cognitive and situational constraints. Repeated, controlled and contextualized evidence is stronger than overinterpreting a single slip.|Performance evidence|Moderate
5|What is a major limitation of claiming a form is impossible because it never appeared in a small corpus?|Absence may reflect the corpus's size and sampling.|Every corpus includes all possible contexts.|The claim is guaranteed by the alphabet.|Frequency and grammatical possibility are identical.|A corpus samples usage. Rare but grammatical constructions may be absent, and judgments or other data can help investigate their status.|Evidence quality|Challenging
5|Which pairing correctly identifies interfaces in a generative model using these labels?|PF: pronunciation-related representation; LF: interpretation-related representation.|PF: paragraph font; LF: letter frequency.|PF: prescriptive form; LF: language family.|PF: past form only; LF: legal formula only.|PF and LF are framework-specific interface terms. The distinction concerns pronunciation and interpretation rather than typography or social standards.|PF and LF|Easy
5|Which statement best separates standardization from grammatical structure?|A standard gains institutional status; nonstandard varieties can still have systematic grammars.|Only a standard variety permits syntactic analysis.|A dialect becomes a grammar only after official recognition.|Standardization removes all language change permanently.|A standard variety serves important social functions, but institutional prestige and structural systematicity are different properties.|Standard and nonstandard varieties|Moderate
6|What is the basic pattern of Sara gave Ali a book in a traditional clause-pattern analysis?|SVOO.|SVC.|SV.|SVA.|Sara is subject, gave verb, Ali indirect object and a book direct object. Two objects do not make the sentence compound.|Clause-pattern analysis|Moderate
6|What is the basic pattern of They made Ali captain?|SVOC.|SVOO.|SVC.|SV.|Ali is object and captain predicates an identity of Ali, so captain is object complement. Compare a true recipient-plus-theme construction such as gave Ali a book.|Clause-pattern analysis|Challenging
6|What is the basic pattern of Sara put the keys on the desk?|SVOA.|SV.|SVC.|SVOO.|Put selects a placement construction with an object and location. On the desk is an adverbial in traditional pattern labels, not a second nominal object.|Clause-pattern analysis|Moderate
6|How many independent clauses occur in Although it rained, Ali stayed and Sara left?|Two.|One.|Three.|Four.|Ali stayed and Sara left are independent coordinated clauses; Although it rained is subordinate. The whole sentence is compound-complex.|Clause counting|Challenging
6|Which sentence remains simple despite containing and?|Ali and Sara laughed.|Ali laughed, and Sara cried.|Ali laughed because Sara arrived.|When Sara arrived, Ali laughed, and Bilal waved.|Ali and Sara is a coordinated subject within one independent clause. The alternatives contain multiple clauses or subordination.|Sentence types|Moderate
6|In The window was broken by Ali, which pairing is correct?|The window: subject; Ali: agent.|Ali: subject; the window: agent.|The window: instrument; Ali: direct object.|Ali: subject complement; the window: source.|Passive structure separates grammatical relations from semantic roles. The patient/theme is subject, while the optional by-phrase expresses the agent.|Syntax and semantics|Challenging
6|Which standard English question correctly uses do-support?|Did Sara leave?|Did Sara left?|Does Sara leaves?|Do Sara left?|The finite auxiliary did carries past tense; leave remains in the base form. Standard third-person singular present would be Does Sara leave? rather than leaves after does.|Question formation|Moderate
6|In I saw the man with binoculars, which reading arises from attaching the PP to saw or the verb phrase?|The binoculars were the means used for seeing.|The man necessarily owned the binoculars.|The binoculars are necessarily the grammatical subject.|The sentence cannot have any interpretation.|Attachment to the seeing event produces an instrumental reading. Attachment inside the noun phrase instead describes the man with binoculars.|Structural ambiguity|Challenging
6|In Lahore, oranges grow has an initial location phrase. Which statement is correct?|The adverbial is fronted but subject still precedes verb.|The verb necessarily precedes the subject.|Oranges is the direct object of grow.|The sentence contains two independent clauses.|Fronting a location does not itself cause subject-verb inversion. Compare a construction such as Here comes the bus, where the verb does precede the subject.|Fronting versus inversion|Challenging
6|Choose the most parallel revision of She enjoys reading and to swim.|She enjoys reading and swimming.|She enjoys to reading and swim.|She enjoys read and to swimming.|She enjoys reading and swims yesterday.|The revision coordinates two -ing activity constructions after enjoys. It matches their grammatical form and preserves a natural complement pattern for the verb.|Parallelism|Easy
7|If every rose is a flower, which inference follows from This is a rose?|This is a flower.|Every flower is a rose.|This cannot be a plant.|Rose and flower are exact synonyms.|Hyponymy supports inference from subtype to superordinate. The converse fails because the category flower also includes non-rose flowers.|Hyponymy and entailment|Moderate
7|Which pair illustrates a part-whole relation rather than a subtype relation?|Wheel and car.|Rose and flower.|Bus and vehicle.|Sparrow and bird.|A wheel is part of a car, not a kind of car. The other pairs are ordinary hyponym-superordinate relations.|Lexical relation discrimination|Moderate
7|What is wrong with inferring cold directly from not hot in every context?|Hot and cold are gradable and allow intermediate temperatures.|Hot and cold must have identical meanings.|Cold is a superordinate of every temperature.|Not hot is always a tautology.|Something can be warm or mild without being hot or cold. Gradable antonyms do not exhaust the scale as an idealized complementary pair would.|Antonymy|Moderate
7|Which classification best fits The university decided to increase fees, when university refers to decision-makers?|Metonymy.|Literal identification of a building's mental state.|Homophony.|A phonemic minimal pair.|An institution label stands for its authorized people or decision-making body through association. This differs from a metaphor based primarily on cross-domain comparison.|Metonymy|Challenging
7|In Sara sent a parcel from Lahore to Karachi, which role is assigned to Karachi?|Goal.|Source.|Instrument.|Experiencer.|Karachi is the destination, hence goal. Lahore is source, the parcel theme, and Sara is the agent in the ordinary interpretation.|Semantic roles|Moderate
7|In Ali feared the storm, which role best describes Ali?|Experiencer.|Instrument.|Goal.|Source.|Fear is a psychological state. Ali experiences it and need not intentionally initiate it, so experiencer is more appropriate than intentional agent.|Semantic roles|Moderate
7|A penguin is judged less typical than a sparrow as a bird. Which conclusion is warranted?|Typicality and category membership can differ.|A penguin is therefore not a bird.|All category members must be equally typical.|The words penguin and bird are antonyms.|Prototype judgments rank representativeness, not necessarily membership. Penguins remain birds despite departing from some familiar expectations such as flight.|Prototype theory|Challenging
7|Which sentence has ordinary syntactic form but violates common literal selectional expectations?|The cupboard drank the soup.|Cupboard the soup drank the.|The child drank the soup.|Did the child drink the soup?|The cupboard drank the soup has familiar subject-verb-object order but assigns drinking to an inanimate item. Personification could make it interpretable in a special context.|Semantic anomaly|Moderate
7|Morning star and evening star refer to Venus but present it differently. What does this show?|Same reference need not mean same sense.|Same reference guarantees identical descriptive content.|Every co-referring expression is a homophone.|Reference depends only on spelling length.|The example distinguishes what an expression refers to from its sense or mode of presentation. A referent alone does not exhaust every aspect of meaning.|Sense and reference|Challenging
7|Sara owns a red car, but she does not own a car is inconsistent in the ordinary sense. Why?|The first clause entails the proposition denied by the second.|Red car and car are unrelated homonyms.|Ownership is a type of phonetic aspiration.|The first clause merely suggests a cancellable social greeting.|The red-car proposition guarantees car ownership under the stated reading. Denying that consequence creates a contradiction, unlike cancelling an ordinary conversational implicature.|Entailment and contradiction|Challenging
8|A says Did all the students pass? B says Some did, perhaps all. Which conclusion is best?|The not-all inference can be cancelled without semantic contradiction.|Some logically entails not all in every ordinary use.|B has contradicted the minimum existential claim.|Quantity applies only to sound volume.|Some conventionally asserts at least some; a not-all suggestion can arise as a scalar implicature. Perhaps all cancels that suggestion while retaining the explicit assertion.|Scalar implicature|Challenging
8|A visitor asks Can you open the window? in a hot room. What is the likely illocutionary act?|A request to open it.|An assertion that it is already open.|A legal declaration transferring ownership.|A promise by the visitor to repair it.|The grammatical form asks about ability, but conventional context supports a request. This is an indirect speech act; a special medical context might instead make ability the actual issue.|Indirect speech acts|Moderate
8|A warning makes a child step away from traffic. Which pairing is correct?|Warning: illocution; stepping away: perlocutionary effect.|Warning: coda; stepping away: onset.|Warning: homograph; stepping away: homophone.|Warning: extension; stepping away: intension.|The warning is the act performed in saying something; the child's response is an effect on behaviour. The effect might fail even if the warning is appropriately performed.|Speech-act layers|Challenging
8|Someone says Sara has stopped smoking. What is normally presupposed?|Sara smoked before the relevant stopping point.|Sara has never smoked.|Sara will necessarily smoke tomorrow.|The speaker personally smokes.|Stop is a change-of-state trigger. The previous smoking is typically backgrounded, although the hearer can challenge the assumption and context can affect projection.|Presupposition|Moderate
8|A person says I will come here tomorrow. Which information is essential to identify the deictic references?|Who speaks, where the deictic location is, and the relevant day of speaking.|The number of letters alone.|Only the dictionary order of come.|The speaker's exact age in every possible case.|I, here and tomorrow respectively involve person, place and time anchors. Conventional meanings alone do not identify a unique speaker, location and date.|Deictic anchoring|Moderate
8|An unauthorized spectator says I sentence you to prison outside a court. What is the central issue?|The institutional felicity conditions are not satisfied.|The sentence has no recognizable words.|Every performative automatically changes legal status.|The utterance contains no finite verb.|An institutional declaration requires appropriate authority and circumstances. A grammatically formed formula is insufficient to produce the legal result.|Felicity conditions|Challenging
8|Asked for the time, a speaker gives a lengthy unrelated history of clocks. Which maxim is most directly challenged by the topic shift?|Relation.|Quality alone.|Voicing.|Phonotactics.|The response fails to address the current purpose of obtaining the time. Excessive length may also raise quantity or manner concerns, but the unrelated topic is primarily a relevance issue.|Maxim discrimination|Moderate
8|During a storm, someone conspicuously says What perfect picnic weather! What is the likely Gricean analysis?|Overt flouting of quality supports an ironic interpretation.|A covert lie intended to establish the weather report as literally true.|A necessary entailment that no storm exists.|An ordinary inflectional suffix.|The obvious mismatch can make literal truth implausible and invite an ironic interpretation. It differs from a concealed attempt to deceive about the actual weather.|Flouting and irony|Challenging
8|What is the safest interpretation of a two-line conversation lacking a clear situation?|Several pragmatic interpretations may remain possible.|There must be exactly one recoverable intention in all contexts.|Context never affects utterance meaning.|Every ambiguous exchange is syntactically impossible.|Pragmatic inference depends on context and assumptions. Without them, an analyst should not invent a single speaker intention and treat it as certain.|Contextual inference|Challenging
8|A hearer responds Wait, you have a sister? to My sister is visiting. What has the hearer done?|Challenged or queried a presupposed background assumption.|Confirmed that every presupposition is always shared.|Performed an official marriage declaration.|Demonstrated that all pronouns are meaningless.|The possessive noun phrase commonly introduces a background assumption that the speaker has a sister. A hearer may question it instead of silently accommodating it.|Presupposition accommodation|Challenging
'''
